---
import { Aside, Steps } from "@astrojs/starlight/components";

interface Props {
  pathMode: "self-service" | "lead-provisioned";
}

const { pathMode } = Astro.props;
const isPathB = pathMode === "lead-provisioned";
---

<Aside type="tip" title="One GitHub repo, two local folders on vm-dev01">
  {isPathB ? (
    <p>
      <strong>Do not create a new GitHub repository.</strong> Reuse the private{" "}
      <code>&lt;org&gt;/&lt;your-repo&gt;</code> student repository listed on your Handoff Card (for
      example <code>&lt;org&gt;/&lt;event&gt;-student-01</code>). On <code>vm-dev01</code>, two
      separate local folders connect to that one GitHub repository for different jobs:
    </p>
  ) : (
    <p>
      <strong>Do not create a new GitHub repository.</strong> Reuse the private{" "}
      <code>&lt;your-org&gt;/&lt;your-repo&gt;</code> repository you created in{" "}
      <a href="/deploy/set-up-your-repo/#11-create-your-repo-everyone">Step 1.1</a>. On{" "}
      <code>vm-dev01</code>, two separate local folders connect to that one GitHub repository for
      different jobs:
    </p>
  )}
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>
          Folder on <code>vm-dev01</code>
        </th>
        <th>Where it comes from</th>
        <th>Branch &amp; remote you push to</th>
        <th>What goes here</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>C:\src\factory</code>
        </td>
        <td>Pre-cloned on the VM from the public kit</td>
        <td>
          <code>vm-dev01-work</code> &rarr; pushes to <code>member</code> (your existing GitHub
          repo)
        </td>
        <td>
          Contoso University app code and <code>.github/upgrades/</code>
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <code>C:\src\&lt;your-repo&gt;</code>
        </td>
        <td>A second local clone of your existing GitHub repo</td>
        <td>
          <code>main</code> &rarr; pushes to <code>origin</code> (that same GitHub repo)
        </td>
        <td>
          Written challenge evidence only (<code>factory\evidence\</code>)
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</Aside>

<p>
  Use two browser windows on your own computer: one for Bastion, the other for the Azure portal and
  this site. Allow portal pop-ups and Bastion clipboard access, and enter sign-in codes on your own
  computer where your passkeys and password manager work. Step 1 below runs in your Dev Container
  (or GitHub Codespace), step 2 runs in your local browser, and steps 3 to 9 run on{" "}
  <code>vm-dev01</code> in <strong>PowerShell 7</strong> (<code>pwsh</code>), never Windows
  PowerShell 5.1.
</p>

<Steps>
  <ol>
    <li>
      {isPathB ? (
        <>
          <p>
            <strong>Read your VM password and repo URL.</strong> In PowerShell in your Dev Container
            or Codespace, from <code>factory/</code>:
          </p>
          <pre><code class="language-powershell">{`$s = Get-Content .local/settings.json | ConvertFrom-Json
(Get-Content ".local/$($s.subscriptionId)/datacenter.json" | ConvertFrom-Json).adminPassword
git remote get-url origin`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            Keep the password for <code>labadmin</code> (which also opens <code>vm-app01</code>) and
            your student repo URL (<code>https://github.com/&lt;org&gt;/&lt;your-repo&gt;.git</code>
            ) handy, along with the <code>&lt;tenant-id&gt;</code> and{" "}
            <code>&lt;workload-subscription-id&gt;</code> from your Handoff Card.
          </p>
        </>
      ) : (
        <>
          <p>
            <strong>Get your VM password, subscription ID and Step 1 repo URL.</strong> In
            PowerShell in your dev container, from <code>factory/</code>:
          </p>
          <pre><code class="language-powershell">{`$s = Get-Content .local/settings.json | ConvertFrom-Json
(Get-Content ".local/$($s.subscriptionId)/datacenter.json" | ConvertFrom-Json).adminPassword
$s.subscriptionId
git remote get-url origin`}</code></pre>
          <p>
            The outputs are the password for <code>labadmin</code> (which also opens{" "}
            <code>vm-app01</code>), your workload subscription ID, and the URL of the private GitHub
            repo you created in Step 1 (
            <code>https://github.com/&lt;your-org&gt;/&lt;your-repo&gt;.git</code>). Keep them
            privately for the next steps. If <code>datacenter.json</code> is missing after a green
            run, run <code>./scripts/Get-PipelineEvidence.ps1</code> from <code>factory/</code>{" "}
            first (or for the standard disposable lab when Arc failed, run{" "}
            <code>./scripts/Initialize-Settings.ps1 -WriteLabCredentials</code>; for custom
            credentials, ask your team lead privately).
          </p>
        </>
      )}
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Connect through Bastion.</strong> In the Azure portal, check your directory and
        workload subscription filter. Open <strong>Resource groups</strong> &gt;{" "}
        <strong>rg-datacenter</strong> &gt; <strong>vm-dev01</strong> &gt; <strong>Connect</strong>{" "}
        &gt; <strong>Bastion</strong>. Choose <strong>Password</strong>, enter <code>labadmin</code>{" "}
        and the password, then <strong>Connect</strong>. After your first sign-in, wait up to five
        minutes for the VS Code extensions to finish installing. On a Windows computer,{" "}
        <code>./scripts/Connect-DatacenterVm.ps1</code> is an optional shortcut (needs PowerShell 7,
        Azure CLI with the <code>bastion</code> extension, and a local copy of <code>factory/</code>
        ).
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Sign in to GitHub and set your git identity.</strong> Open{" "}
        <strong>
          PowerShell 7 (<code>pwsh</code>)
        </strong>{" "}
        on <code>vm-dev01</code>:
      </p>
      <pre><code class="language-powershell">{`gh auth login --web
gh auth setup-git
$u = gh api user | ConvertFrom-Json
git config --global user.name ($u.name ?? $u.login)
git config --global user.email "$($u.id)+$($u.login)@users.noreply.github.com"`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Choose <strong>GitHub.com</strong>, <strong>HTTPS</strong> and <strong>Yes</strong> to
        authenticate Git. Enter the one-time code at <code>https://github.com/login/device</code> on
        your own computer. <code>gh auth setup-git</code> lets pushes use that sign-in.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Sign in to Azure with your own identity.</strong> Still in <code>pwsh</code> on{" "}
        <code>vm-dev01</code>:
      </p>
      {isPathB ? (
        <pre><code class="language-powershell">{`az login --tenant '<tenant-id>' --use-device-code
az account set --subscription '<workload-subscription-id>'
az account show --query name -o tsv
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('AZURE_TOKEN_CREDENTIALS', 'AzureCliCredential', 'User')`}</code></pre>
      ) : (
        <pre><code class="language-powershell">{`az login --use-device-code
az account set --subscription '<workload-subscription-id>'
az account show --query name -o tsv
[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('AZURE_TOKEN_CREDENTIALS', 'AzureCliCredential', 'User')`}</code></pre>
      )}
      <p>
        Enter the code at <code>https://microsoft.com/devicelogin</code> (or run{" "}
        <code>az login</code> in Bastion if your tenant blocks device code sign-in). Confirm the
        subscription name matches your workload subscription. Setting{" "}
        <code>AZURE_TOKEN_CREDENTIALS=AzureCliCredential</code> makes local app runs use your own
        Entra data roles instead of the VM&apos;s system-assigned identity. Open VS Code only{" "}
        <em>after</em> setting this variable, and never set it on App Service.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Refresh the pre-cloned kit folder once before creating your work branch:</strong>
      </p>
      <pre><code class="language-powershell">{`cd C:\\src\\factory
git switch main
git pull`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>C:\src\factory</code> is already pre-cloned on <code>vm-dev01</code> from the public
        kit. If the folder is missing, ask your team lead before the event or your coach on event
        days; if they ask you to recreate it, run this sparse clone so answer keys and documentation
        tooling stay out:
      </p>
      <pre><code class="language-powershell">{`git clone --no-checkout https://github.com/jonathan-vella/apex-factory-hackathon.git C:\\src\\factory
cd C:\\src\\factory
git sparse-checkout set --no-cone '/*' '!/coach/' '!/facilitator/' '!/docs/' '!/site/' '!/test/' '!/.vale/' '!/.vale.ini' '!/.markdownlint-cli2.mjs' '!/.prettierignore' '!/prettier.config.mjs' '!/lefthook.yml' '!/package.json' '!/package-lock.json' '!/.node-version' '!/.nvmrc'
git checkout`}</code></pre>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>
          Point a <code>member</code> remote at your existing GitHub repo and push{" "}
          <code>vm-dev01-work</code> (once):
        </strong>{" "}
        use the exact repository URL from step 1—do not create a new repository on GitHub:
      </p>
      <pre><code class="language-powershell">{isPathB
  ? `cd C:\\src\\factory
git remote add member https://github.com/<org>/<your-repo>.git
git switch -c vm-dev01-work
git push -u member vm-dev01-work
git remote -v
git branch -vv`
  : `cd C:\\src\\factory
git remote add member https://github.com/<your-org>/<your-repo>.git
git switch -c vm-dev01-work
git push -u member vm-dev01-work
git remote -v
git branch -vv`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>origin</code> stays the public kit and <code>member</code> points to your existing
        private GitHub repo. <code>vm-dev01-work</code> tracks <code>member/vm-dev01-work</code>.
        Never push to <code>origin</code>, never run <code>git pull</code> in{" "}
        <code>C:\src\factory</code> after this setup, and never merge <code>vm-dev01-work</code>{" "}
        into <code>main</code> (their folder layouts differ; see{" "}
        <a href="/guides/ghcp-upgrade/#save-your-work-and-keep-the-branches-straight">
          Save your work and keep the branches straight
        </a>
        ).
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>
          Clone that same GitHub repo into <code>C:\src\&lt;your-repo&gt;</code> for evidence:
        </strong>{" "}
        make a local clone of your existing private repo alongside <code>C:\src\factory</code>:
      </p>
      <pre><code class="language-powershell">{isPathB
  ? `git clone https://github.com/<org>/<your-repo>.git C:\\src\\<your-repo>`
  : `git clone https://github.com/<your-org>/<your-repo>.git C:\\src\\<your-repo>`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        This local folder stays on <code>main</code> for files under <code>factory\evidence\</code>;
        application code changes stay in <code>C:\src\factory</code> on <code>vm-dev01-work</code>.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Open VS Code on the kit root and sign in to Copilot:</strong>
      </p>
      <pre><code class="language-powershell">{`code C:\\src\\factory`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        Trust the authors when VS Code asks. Choose the <strong>Accounts</strong> icon &gt;{" "}
        <strong>Sign in with GitHub to use GitHub Copilot</strong> and complete browser sign-in.
        Open <code>C:\src\factory</code> (not <code>app/</code>) so the agent finds{" "}
        <code>.github/skills/</code> and <code>.github/modernization/</code>.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Configure the Upgrade agent.</strong> In <strong>Extensions</strong>, enable{" "}
        <strong>GitHub Copilot upgrade</strong>; disable{" "}
        <strong>GitHub Copilot modernization</strong> for the workspace if present. In Chat, choose{" "}
        <strong>Local</strong> for <strong>Session Target</strong> (not the remote coding agent
        harness). Press <code>F1</code> &gt; <strong>MCP: List Servers</strong> &gt;{" "}
        <strong>Upgrade</strong> &gt; <strong>Configure Model Access</strong> and allow the models
        you will use. Confirm the agent picker lists <strong>Upgrade</strong>, then send{" "}
        <code>&quot;hello&quot;</code>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</Steps>
